          { name: "Majority-Minority Community Economic Mobility", tag: "lean-into", why: "CT-HD-5 is a 69.87% majority-minority district (47.61% Black, 21.26% Hispanic, 5.58% Asian) with a median household income of $81,954 and 30.6% college attainment — solid middle-class fundamentals that mask persistent economic stratification within communities of color. The 38.7% renter rate signals a constituency with limited asset accumulation despite household incomes above the state median, indicating that homeownership and intergenerational wealth-building barriers are the operative economic concerns. A representative who speaks specifically to closing the homeownership gap, supporting small business formation in minority communities, and ensuring equitable access to credit and professional networks addresses the material economic anxiety that drives voter behavior in majority-minority districts." },
          { name: "Education & Workforce Pipeline for Young Adult Constituency", tag: "lean-into", why: "The 36.6-year median age and 30.6% college attainment rate signal a district with significant young adult presence and room for educational advancement. With nearly 39% of residents as renters — predominantly younger households — and a household income of $81,954, this is a constituency evaluating on whether education and job training investments are producing tangible pathways to economic stability and homeownership. School funding adequacy, higher education affordability, and credential-to-career pipeline alignment are not abstract policy issues but concrete determinants of whether young adults in CT-HD-5 can build stable economic lives. A legislator who leads on K-12 adequacy, workforce development, and student debt relief is responding to the actual economic trajectory concerns of this district's demographic majority." },
          { name: "Racial Equity & Institutional Accountability", tag: "careful", why: "The 100% Democratic result in 2024 reflects not only partisan preference but an expectation of explicit racial equity commitment from the representative. In a 69.87% majority-minority district, performance on policing accountability, hiring equity in state-contracted work, health disparities reduction, and anti-discrimination enforcement is not peripheral to constituent evaluation — it is central. Khan's unopposed status masks a critical vulnerability: the 100% result is a mandate, not a guarantee of reelection if the representative's record on racial equity appears performative or insufficiently aggressive. Any challenger credibly to the left on racial justice implementation would activate this constituency in ways the current margin does not reflect." },
          { name: "Treating the District as Politically 'Settled'", tag: "avoid", why: "A 100% result in any district is electorally fragile precisely because it creates no institutional pressure on the incumbent. In majority-minority urban districts, 100% margins often reflect strong performance on identity and equity issues combined with lack of viable alternatives — not durable attachment to the incumbent. If Khan's record on education funding, police accountability, or housing equity is perceived as insufficient by younger voters or community leaders, a primary challenge is arithmetically viable even in a heavily Democratic seat. The representative must govern as though the 100% is provisional, not permanent." },
          { name: "Framing Majority-Minority Status as a Demographic Characteristic Rather Than a Governance Mandate", tag: "avoid", why: "CT-HD-5's 69.87% majority-minority composition is not a demographic curiosity — it is a statement about whose economic security and institutional treatment matter in this district. Any legislator who treats racial equity, anti-discrimination enforcement, and equitable resource allocation as special-interest positioning rather than basic governance framework will find that framing actively hostile to the lived reality of most constituents. The district's demographic majority expects representation that names and addresses racial inequity explicitly; framing such engagement as 'identity politics' rather than core constituent service is a path to political isolation." }
