          { name: "Demographic Coalition Management Across Five Distinct Communities", tag: "lean-into", why: "CT-HD-9 has no demographic majority: white 47%, Hispanic/Latino 21%, Black 18%, Asian 9%, and ~5% AIAN together create a genuinely plural district where authentic representation requires specific engagement with each community's material concerns. The unopposed 2024 result provides no signal about whether Rojas has built durable relationships with the Black, Hispanic, and Asian communities or whether those voters simply defaulted to Democratic alignment without seeing specific policy responsiveness. At $86,337 median income, all these communities face real concerns around housing costs, educational access, and economic mobility that demand more than generic appeals. A representative who names specific policy commitments to each community—not as separate constituencies but as interconnected parts of the district—builds the coalition durability that survives challenger emergence." },
          { name: "Renter-Majority Housing & Economic Security", tag: "lean-into", why: "The 31.6% renter rate is substantially higher than the state average and signals a significant proportion of younger, mobile, non-homeowner constituents whose political participation and economic concerns differ from homeowner bases. In a $86,337-median-income district, housing costs are almost certainly consuming 30%+ of renter household budgets, creating acute affordability pressure. Unopposed victory often allows incumbents to neglect renter constituencies, assuming their votes will default to the incumbent party; this is precisely how seemingly safe seats become vulnerable when a challenger emerges with specific housing affordability proposals. Building durable support among renters requires tenant protection advocacy, zoning reform that permits density and affordability, and acknowledgment that young renters' economic mobility depends on housing policy specifically." },
          { name: "Sustaining Unopposed Advantage Without Building Durable Constituencies", tag: "careful", why: "The 100% unopposed result in 2024 is both a strategic advantage and a concealed vulnerability: it provided no opportunity to test coalition strength, identify emerging opposition, or gather intelligence about which constituents feel unrepresented. If opposition research is currently being conducted against Rojas by potential 2026 or 2028 challengers, the absence of 2024 opposition means that research is gathering without the incumbent's knowledge or counter-narrative. A diverse district at $86,337 income with 31.6% renters and no single demographic majority is precisely the kind of seat where a well-resourced challenger with specific policy focus on housing affordability, minority economic opportunity, or middle-class tax fairness could create an opening. The strategic task is to move from unopposed advantage to durable coalition—not through complacency but through active constituent engagement." },
          { name: "Assuming Democratic Default in a Plural District", tag: "avoid", why: "The demographic plurality of CT-HD-9 means no single community has overwhelming partisan loyalty; Black voters lean Democratic but respond to specific representation; Hispanic voters in Connecticut often track with economic messaging more than party affiliation; Asian and white voters are genuinely persuadable based on specific policy responsiveness. Treating this district as a Democratic lock that will vote as a bloc regardless of incumbent performance is precisely the assumption that creates openings for challengers. A representative who believes 47% white + 21% Hispanic + 18% Black + 9% Asian = automatic Democratic support is governing without understanding that each community evaluates representation independently." },
          { name: "Treating Unopposed Victory as Evidence of Secure Majority Support", tag: "avoid", why: "The 2024 unopposed result tells us that no Republican challenger emerged with sufficient resources or confidence to contest the seat—not that Rojas has built unshakeable support across the district's diverse communities. In a median-income district with high renter rates and multiple demographic communities, unopposed victory is consistent with either genuine constituent satisfaction or with the absence of competitive investment by the opposition. Any incumbent who interprets unopposed victory as evidence of durable coalition support rather than as incomplete information is strategically vulnerable to a credible challenger who emerges with specific policy proposals and adequate resources." }
