        issues: [
          { name: "Education Policy & Quality-of-Life Governance", tag: "lean-into", why: "With 47.7% college-educated adults, HD-13 has among the state's highest educational attainment, and this constituency evaluates representatives primarily on school funding, teacher compensation, curriculum quality, and higher education access. For college-educated voters, education policy is not an abstract value—it is the primary proxy for whether government is competent and aligned with their interests. A representative who speaks with specificity about education funding mechanisms, teacher recruitment and retention, and the pathway from K-12 to higher education commands the credibility this constituency's material stake in educational excellence demands." },
          { name: "Housing Affordability & Renter Constituencies", tag: "lean-into", why: "The 40.3% renter rate is the highest signal in the district and indicates that housing affordability and tenant protection remain active governance concerns even in a $92,941 median-income community. For younger professionals, families, and lower-income residents in HD-13, housing costs are consuming income that might otherwise fund other priorities, and renter protections are direct material concerns. A representative who can articulate specific housing policy—rent stabilization mechanisms, eviction prevention, landlord regulation, and the relationship between housing costs and economic mobility—connects to a substantial constituency whose Democratic voting reflects their perception that Democratic governance is addressing their housing security." },
          { name: "Racial & Ethnic Minority Engagement in a Diverse District", tag: "careful", why: "With 18.08% Hispanic, 15.15% Black, and 6.65% Asian residents, HD-13 is a meaningfully diverse district where no single group forms a majority and where the 66% Democratic margin reflects genuine cross-community coalition-building. The assumption that voters of color automatically default to the Democratic candidate is both arithmetically false—the 34% Republican share includes some minority voters—and politically dangerous, as it invites under-mobilization or erosion to a credible opponent who actually engages these communities. Any candidate who wants to consolidate the 66% must treat each community's specific economic and social concerns—immigrant integration for the Hispanic community, economic mobility and criminal justice for the Black community, professional integration and civil rights for the Asian community—as active governing priorities, not check-box acknowledgments." },
          { name: "Treating the 66% Margin as Stable Without Active Consolidation", tag: "avoid", why: "A 66% result is dominant but not invulnerable, particularly in a district with 40.3% renters and substantial racial and ethnic diversity where constituent engagement requires active, ongoing presence. Any candidate or incumbent who assumes that college-educated, economically secure urban voters will automatically vote Democratic while neglecting renters, younger professionals, or minority communities is not consolidating the actual coalition that produced 66%—and is leaving the margin vulnerable to erosion in the next competitive cycle." },
          { name: "Adopting Messaging Designed to Appeal Beyond the District's Actual Composition", tag: "avoid", why: "HD-13's Democratic alignment is structural and rooted in its educated, diverse, urban profile—not marginal or in need of persuasion. A candidate who campaigns as though the seat requires appealing to conservative-leaning swing voters or adopts positioning designed to attract the Republican 34% underperforms the district's actual composition and signals to the college-educated, diverse, and renter constituencies that their concerns are not the campaign's primary focus. This messaging error—however well-intentioned toward statewide positioning—directly depresses turnout and vote share among the constituencies that actually won the seat." }
        ],
        memoHeadline: "Connecticut HD-13: A 66-34 Democratic Stronghold Built on College Education, Diverse Urbanity, and Economic Security—Where the Winning Strategy Is Consolidation, Not Expansion.",
        memoParagraphs: [
          "CT-HD-13 is a decisively Democratic district that Doucette won with a commanding 66% in 2024—a 32-point margin in a state where such spreads are reserved for seats with fundamentally uncompetitive partisan character. The district's demographic profile explains this outcome with clarity: 47.7% college-educated adults (among the highest in the state), a $92,941 median household income reflecting upper-middle-class economic security, and a median age of 38.4 that signals a settled professional constituency. The 60.79% white population, combined with meaningful presence from Hispanic (18.08%), Black (15.15%), and Asian (6.65%) communities, produces a diverse urban or inner-ring suburban profile where educational attainment and economic stability correlate strongly with Democratic preference. The 40.3% renter rate—the highest signal of transience or younger household formation in the district—suggests a constituency that includes both established homeowners and younger professionals, both of whom have voted Democratic consistently in recent cycles.",
          "The strategic challenge for any candidate in HD-13 is not to win it—the seat's Democratic alignment is structural and durable—but to understand what holds it and avoid the erosion that can occur when a dominant incumbent takes consolidation for granted or when an open seat candidate misreads which constituencies actually delivered the 66% margin. The college-educated, economically secure, and racially diverse character of this district means that voters evaluate on education policy, quality-of-life governance, and whether the representative is genuinely engaged with the community's actual demographic composition. The renter rate of 40.3% signals that housing affordability and tenant protection remain active concerns even in a relatively prosperous district; younger professionals and families are not simply defaulting to Democratic voting—they are choosing it because they perceive Democratic governance as addressing their material circumstances. Any candidate who wins this seat by consolidating the college-educated homeowner base while neglecting the renter, younger, or minority constituencies will find the 66% margin erode more quickly than expected in the next cycle. The inverse problem—a candidate who campaigns as though the seat requires persuasion or who adopts messaging designed to appeal to voters outside this district's actual composition—will underperform the structural Democratic advantage and leave votes on the table."
        ],
        memoBullets: [
          "Consolidate the college-educated, economically secure base through education policy specificity: public school funding, teacher compensation, and higher education access are not abstract values for 47.7% college-educated voters—they are direct material interests and the primary proxy for whether government is delivering for people like them.",
          "Engage the 40.3% renter constituency with concrete housing policy: rent stabilization, landlord regulation, eviction prevention, and the relationship between housing costs and economic mobility are the material concerns driving younger and lower-income households in a district where even economic security has limits.",
          "Maintain active constituent presence across the 18.08% Hispanic, 15.15% Black, and 6.65% Asian communities: in a diverse district where no single racial or ethnic group is a majority, the 66% margin reflects genuine cross-community coalition-building that requires ongoing engagement, not assumption that Democratic preference is automatic.",
          "Avoid positioning this seat as requiring persuasion or appealing to conservative-leaning swing voters: HD-13's Democratic alignment is structural, and candidates who adopt messaging designed to appeal to the Republican 34% underperform the district's actual composition and leave the college-educated, diverse, and renter constituencies under-mobilized."
        ]
