          { name: "Educational & Institutional Quality", tag: "lean-into", why: "With 40.1% college attainment and a 47.5-year median age, HD-15 is a constituency that places high stakes on school quality, higher education access, healthcare systems, and the overall quality of public institutions. Democratic governance that visibly invests in these institutions—and can demonstrate outcomes in student achievement, healthcare access, and community infrastructure—commands the support of an educated, professionally engaged constituency. Conversely, perceived underinvestment in schools or deterioration of public service quality is the most likely driver of Democratic erosion in a district this educated and this affluent." },
          { name: "Renter vs. Homeowner Constituency Divergence", tag: "careful", why: "The 29.5% renter rate in an affluent district signals two distinct economic constituencies with potentially different priorities: established homeowners focused on property tax fairness and housing values, and younger professional renters concerned with affordability, mobility, and access to opportunity. At 81% Democratic support, both groups are currently consolidated, but Democratic messaging that speaks only to homeowner interests (property tax protection, housing stability) risks appearing indifferent to renter economic security. Recognizing and addressing these distinct concerns—affordability for renters, stability for homeowners—maintains the coalition rather than allowing divergence." },
          { name: "Treating 81% as a Floor Rather Than a Consolidation Opportunity", tag: "avoid", why: "An 81% Democratic result is a supermajority that reflects genuine constituency satisfaction with Democratic governance, not a baseline to defend against Republican competition. Any Democratic candidate or incumbent who treats this margin as a victory to protect rather than a constituency to deepen will find engagement declining and alternative challengers emerging from within Democratic circles. The strategic task is not defending against Republicans but ensuring that Democratic governance is visibly delivering on the material concerns (education, healthcare, economic opportunity) that drove this supermajority in the first place." },
          { name: "Assuming Partisan Loyalty Replaces Constituent Engagement", tag: "avoid", why: "A 52% Black district with high income and education levels is Democratic because Democratic governance is perceived as protective of and responsive to Black professional interests—not because Black voters have no alternative or lack information. In a district this educated and this affluent, sustained constituent engagement, visible investment in community institutions, and specific focus on education and healthcare quality are necessary to maintain the 81% result. Taking the vote for granted or assuming partisan alignment will overcome governance underperformance is the primary path to losing a supermajority-level seat to a more responsive Democratic challenger." }
