          { name: "High-Quality Education Funding and Equity", tag: "lean-into", why: "The 44.3-year median age and 66.2% college attainment indicate a constituency of established professionals and parents in peak family-formation years for whom education quality and K-12 funding are direct quality-of-life concerns. This district's residents send children to both public and private schools; they care about whether their substantial property tax contributions are producing competitive public education, and whether state education funding mechanisms are fair and adequate. Demonstrating competence on education policy, funding equity, and the relationship between school quality and property values is essential to holding this constituency's support." },
          { name: "Credible Opposition Capacity from Professional-Class Challengers", tag: "careful", why: "The unopposed 2024 result should not be read as indicating weak opposition infrastructure — it may indicate the opposite: that potential challengers concluded the incumbent was genuinely strong enough to defeat, and chose not to mount a costly primary or general election campaign. In affluent suburban districts with high college attainment and concentrated professional-class voters, a credible challenger articulating specific, competent solutions on property tax relief, housing affordability, or education funding could rapidly erode the current 100% position. The incumbent's margin is not a floor; it is a contingent position dependent on continued perceived governance competence." },
          { name: "Taking the Unopposed Victory as Evidence of Political Invulnerability", tag: "avoid", why: "A 100% unopposed result in 2024 does not indicate a durable supermajority or a district incapable of producing opposition. It indicates that no organized challenger ran in that particular cycle — a fact consistent with either strong incumbent positioning or strategic decisions by potential challengers to focus resources elsewhere. In a district of affluent, educated, asset-holding suburban voters, the absence of opposition in one cycle has no predictive power for the next cycle if circumstances change or a credible alternative candidate emerges." },
          { name: "Neglecting the Professional-Class Asian and Latino Communities", tag: "avoid", why: "At 9.75% Asian and 8.37% Hispanic/Latino, CT-HD-31 has meaningful professional-class and college-educated non-white constituencies whose political engagement and support cannot be assumed even in a deep-blue district. These communities often have specific concerns around education equity, economic mobility, healthcare access, and whether state governance is responsive to their needs — and their support should be actively consolidated, not taken for granted. A challenger who demonstrates specific competence on issues affecting these communities could erode margins among professional-class voters regardless of partisan registration." }
