          { name: "Aging-in-Place & Healthcare Access in an Older Suburban Community", tag: "lean-into", why: "The 42.7-year median age, combined with 79.3% homeownership and $110,503 income, describes a cohort of homeowners who have built asset wealth over decades and face concrete questions about healthcare sustainability, Medicare adequacy, and long-term care planning as they age. Connecticut's high healthcare costs and limited rural hospital network in some areas create real economic vulnerability for older homeowners on fixed income trajectories. A legislative agenda addressing healthcare cost control, prescription drug affordability, and the state's role in ensuring Medicare-adequate coverage resonates directly with this community's material life stage concerns." },
          { name: "Demographic Diversity & Equitable Service Access in an Affluent District", tag: "careful", why: "While CT-HD-32 is 79.79% white with high median income, it contains meaningful Hispanic (6.82%), Asian (5.16%), and Black (3.91%) populations whose economic and social integration into the district's affluence is not guaranteed and whose concerns around equitable access to education, housing, and economic opportunity can become invisible in a district-level strategy focused only on the white homeowner majority. The 18.9% renter rate, while low in absolute terms, represents approximately one-fifth of households without property wealth and potentially higher economic precarity than the homeowner-dominant narrative reflects. Authentic engagement with these communities requires specific attention to equity in school resource allocation, housing policy, and economic mobility pathways rather than assuming affluence distributes evenly across all demographic groups." },
          { name: "Ideological Positioning or Culture-War Framing", tag: "avoid", why: "An unopposed 2024 result (100% Republican with no Democratic opponent) is arithmetically inevitable, not an indicator of genuine political consensus. In a district with 47.2% college education, $110,503 income, and meaningful demographic diversity, abstract ideological messaging or culture-war positioning will resonate primarily with committed partisans while potentially alienating affluent suburban professionals whose political engagement is often conditional on specific competence and local governance quality rather than partisan loyalty. The vacuum of opposition suggests opportunity for a challenger who contests the seat on substance, not a safe majority that requires no adjustment." },
          { name: "Taking the District's Affluence & Stability for Granted", tag: "avoid", why: "Connecticut's economy faces real structural pressure — aging infrastructure, rising healthcare costs, and competitive pressure on corporate headquarters and professional services. An affluent suburban district with 79% homeownership is particularly vulnerable to economic disruption that erodes property values or makes current tax burdens unsustainable for fixed-income retirees. A representative who treats the district's current affluence as permanent and stable rather than as something that requires active governance investment in education quality, infrastructure, and economic competitiveness risks being blindsided by a challenger who speaks to economic anxiety that affluence masks but does not eliminate." }
