        issues: [
          { name: "Renter-Majority Economic Security & Housing Affordability", tag: "lean-into", why: "At 53.6% renters with $58,594 median household income, the majority of HD-46 is economically vulnerable to housing cost volatility and has no property-ownership buffer against inflation. Housing affordability, eviction prevention, tenant protections, and the cost of living are not peripheral quality-of-life issues — they are the material foundation of constituent satisfaction. Any candidate who treats housing as an abstract policy area rather than the primary economic security concern for the district's majority will fail to consolidate the vote share required to hold this seat against organized opposition." },
          { name: "Latino Constituency Economic Delivery & Specificity", tag: "lean-into", why: "At 22.88%, Latino voters represent the largest demographic constituency in HD-46 and are essential to any winning coalition. Connecticut Latino voters evaluate on concrete economic outcomes — wage policy, job quality, immigration enforcement specificity, and whether government delivers for their families — rather than on inherited partisan loyalty or symbolic representation. Candidates who engage this constituency with substance on working-class economic issues and community-specific policy solutions build durable support; those who rely on Democratic-brand loyalty alone risk losing engagement when material conditions deteriorate." },
          { name: "Understanding the 34% Republican Coalition", tag: "careful", why: "In a blue state, one-third of the district choosing Republican in 2024 signals either ideologically committed opposition or persuadable swing voters whose concerns are not being addressed by the Democratic incumbent. The strategic question is whether this 34% represents a stable Republican base or voters frustrated with specific aspects of the current representative's agenda — likely around taxes, regulation, or immigration enforcement. Identifying and addressing the material drivers of Republican support, rather than assuming it is simply ideological opposition, is essential to preventing the erosion of the 66% margin if a credible challenger invests in persuasion." },
          { name: "Treating the 66% Margin as a Durable Floor", tag: "avoid", why: "A 66-34 result is substantial but not a foundation for complacency. The district's renter majority, Latino plurality, younger median age (36.3), and low college attainment (20.6%) all indicate a constituency whose partisan preference is driven by economic delivery rather than inherited loyalty. If the incumbent treats the 66% as permission to reduce constituent engagement, or fails to actively serve renter needs and Latino economic concerns, a well-resourced challenger can organize an alternative coalition around these same material issues." },
          { name: "Defaulting to Partisan Messaging Without Material Specificity", tag: "avoid", why: "In a working-class renter-majority district at $58,594 income with 20.6% college attainment, abstract partisan appeals to party identity or ideological positioning fail to resonate with constituents evaluating on whether government is delivering housing, wages, and jobs. Candidates who rely on 'Democrats care about you' messaging without translating that into specific policy proposals for rent regulation, wage enforcement, or job quality will find their support soft among renters and Latinos who have material reasons to defect if the opposition offers concrete alternatives." }
        ],
        memoHeadline: "Connecticut HD-46: A 66-34 Democratic Stronghold with Latino Plurality, Low College Attainment, and Renter-Majority Economics Demanding Constituency-Specific Retention Strategy.",
        memoParagraphs: [
          "CT HD-46 is a solidly Democratic district (66-34 in 2024) whose electoral stability masks significant demographic complexity and potential vulnerability if the incumbent's coalition fractures. The district's median household income of $58,594 and 20.6% college attainment rate signal a working-class to lower-middle-class constituency without the educational credentials or income cushion that typically produce durable partisan loyalty across economic cycles. At 53.6% renters — one of the highest rates in any competitive state legislative district — the majority of HD-46 households lack property ownership, meaning they are economically mobile, politically volatile, and directly exposed to housing costs, rental market dynamics, and cost-of-living pressures that partisan messaging often misses. The 66% Democratic margin is substantial, but it reflects Wilson's current coalition, not necessarily a floor. Understanding why one-third of the district chose the Republican alternative in a blue state, and what specific material concerns drive that choice, is the prerequisite for holding this seat against a credible challenger.",
          "The district's demographic composition — 49.54% white, 22.88% Hispanic/Latino, 13.87% Black, 8.35% Asian, with ~5.36% unaccounted for (likely Native American or multiracial) — means HD-46 is effectively a plurality-Latino district with a white-plurality baseline. Latino voters in Connecticut represent a swing constituency whose partisan preference is driven by economic security, immigration policy specificity, and whether government is delivering tangible material benefits — not inherited Democratic alignment. At a median age of 36.3 and a renter rate of 53.6%, the district skews younger, economically precarious, and sensitive to housing affordability, wages, and job quality. Wilson won decisively at 66%, but that margin depends on consolidating Latino voters, younger renters, and Black constituents around a specific message of economic delivery rather than relying on demographic Democrats to turn out automatically. In a state where Democratic margins are generally safe, the real strategic question is whether the current representative is actively serving the material needs of renters, working-class Latinos, and younger adults — or whether complacency around a 32-point margin masks declining engagement with the constituency that actually elected him."
        ],
        memoBullets: [
          "Lead with renter-specific economic policy: housing affordability, tenant protections, wage enforcement, and cost-of-living assistance are not peripheral issues for a 53.6% renter majority at $58,594 median income — they are the material foundation of constituent satisfaction and retention. Make concrete, specific commitments on rent control, eviction prevention, and housing development that prioritizes affordability rather than abstract pro-housing framing.",
          "Engage Latino voters with economic substance, not symbolism: the 22.88% Latino plurality is Wilson's largest constituency, but Latino voters in Connecticut evaluate on wage policy, job quality, immigration enforcement specificity, and whether government delivers for their families — not inherited Democratic loyalty. Build deep relationships with Latino community organizations, small business owners, and workers in the district's dominant service and construction sectors.",
          "Identify and address the 34% Republican coalition: in a blue state, one-third of HD-46 choosing Republican signals either specific policy disagreement (likely around taxes, regulation, or immigration) or disenfranchisement from the Democratic agenda. Understanding whether this third represents ideologically committed Republicans or persuadable swing voters frustrated with the incumbent will determine whether the 66-34 margin is durable or fragile.",
          "Avoid treating the 66% result as a floor: a two-thirds majority is substantial, but it depends on active constituent engagement across renters, Latinos, young adults, and Black voters — constituencies that will organize an alternative if they conclude the incumbent is not delivering on housing, wages, and economic opportunity. This is a seat that demands continuous relationship-building, not the quiet confidence of a landslide."
        ]
