          { name: "Healthcare Access and Affordability for Professionals", tag: "lean-into", why: "A professionally employed constituency at $112,745 median income faces real healthcare concerns—prescription drug costs, insurance adequacy, access to specialists—that are not addressed by generic partisan positioning on healthcare. Candidates who offer specific solutions (e.g., prescription drug price negotiation, insurance transparency, mental health parity) will resonate more effectively with voters who have the income and education to evaluate policy detail and hold representatives accountable for delivery on stated commitments." },
          { name: "Fiscal Management and Property Tax Fairness", tag: "careful", why: "A homeowner-dominant district (80.4%) at high income levels contains voters who prioritize both adequate public funding and fiscal responsibility; the tension between these concerns is real and requires nuance rather than simple anti-tax positioning. In a 51-49 district, candidates who acknowledge that sustainable public services require adequate revenue, while also articulating specific concerns about tax efficiency and spending accountability, better reflect this constituency's actual views than those who campaign on tax cuts alone." },
          { name: "Climate and Environmental Policy as a Differentiator", tag: "avoid", why: "While climate concerns are present in college-educated constituencies, treating climate and environmental policy as a primary organizing message in a 51-49 affluent suburban district will not change the partisan result and will alienate the business-owner and property-rights segments of the Republican coalition without offsetting gains among swing voters. The focus should remain on local quality-of-life issues (school funding, healthcare) where policy specificity differentiates candidates rather than abstract environmental positioning." },
          { name: "Anti-Government or Anti-Regulation Rhetoric Without Specificity", tag: "avoid", why: "In a high-income, educated district, generic anti-government messaging will not resonate with a constituency that depends on quality public schools, professional licensing bodies, and regulatory frameworks that protect property values and consumer safety. Candidates who campaign on 'smaller government' without specifying which services they would reduce or which regulations they would eliminate will be challenged by voters who understand that many government functions they value are on the chopping block—and who will vote accordingly." }
