          "The strategic imperative for the Democratic incumbent is not to chase expansion into Republican territory — the 26% Republican floor is both mathematically manageable and consistent with national patterns of GOP performance in college-educated, renter-majority districts — but rather to consolidate the existing coalition and solve the turnout and engagement problem embedded in the district's youth-heavy, mobile population. A median age of 21.0 signals a constituency where a substantial portion is in transition: students, recent graduates, young professionals in entry-level positions, and renters between housing situations. These voters have lower propensity to turn out in non-presidential years, higher rates of address changes that affect voter registration, and less attachment to place-based political organization than older, homeowner constituencies. The 74% result reflects strong performance among this demographic in 2024, but holding this margin in the 2026 midterm — when overall turnout drops, young voter participation falls, and address changes compound registration challenges — requires a legislative agenda that speaks to the material concerns of young renters and college-educated workers: student debt, housing affordability, wages for entry-level professionals, and healthcare access. Without active engagement of this constituency's real concerns, the 74% can erode materially even if the seat remains safely Democratic."
