          { name: "Healthcare Access & Cost for Aging Population", tag: "lean-into", why: "The district's 49.5-year median age combined with $81,450 income positions healthcare affordability and access as a direct household-budget concern, not an abstract policy issue. Provider availability, prescription drug costs, and long-term care sustainability are the practical governance questions that Connecticut residents in this demographic are evaluating — and where legislative action demonstrates responsiveness to constituent material needs." },
          { name: "Unopposed Victory as Electoral Baseline, Not Ceiling", tag: "careful", why: "Running unopposed in 2024 reflects the absence of organized Democratic opposition, not the absence of Democratic voters or underlying constituent dissatisfaction. In a district this affluent and politically engaged, the opponent did not simply vanish — they were not mobilized because no one articulated a message that activated their concerns. A future challenger who leads with specific answers to healthcare costs, property tax burden, or housing affordability could find substantially more traction than recent partisan patterns suggest, particularly if the incumbent treats 2024 as proof of durability rather than an opportunity to build deeper constituent relationships." },
          { name: "Treating Demographic Homogeneity as Electoral Certainty", tag: "avoid", why: "At 82.4% white with 29.2% college attainment in Connecticut — a state with active demographic change and increasing political sophistication among younger voters — assuming that demographic composition alone guarantees electoral safety is strategically reckless. Connecticut's political environment rewards candidates who engage constituent concerns with specificity; an incumbent who defaults to demographic assumptions rather than active constituent service is vulnerable to a challenger who demonstrates responsiveness to material household concerns." },
          { name: "Assuming Homeowner Dominance Equals Electoral Stability", tag: "avoid", why: "The 76% homeownership rate creates a politically powerful constituency, but homeowners are not a monolithic voting bloc — they are households making real decisions about property taxes, healthcare costs, and municipal services. An incumbent who treats homeowner dominance as permission to ignore specific property tax policy, healthcare access, or local service delivery will find that assumption tested in a cycle where an organized opposition emerges and offers concrete alternatives." }
