          { name: "Property Tax Fairness & Municipal Fiscal Efficiency", tag: "lean-into", why: "At $99,643 median income with 79.5% homeownership, property tax policy and how municipal budgets are managed are direct financial concerns for a constituency with substantial real estate wealth at stake. Tax rate fairness, spending efficiency, infrastructure maintenance, and whether government is delivering services proportional to tax burden are the metrics by which affluent homeowners evaluate local governance. Clear communication about tax policy and fiscal discipline resonates directly with this demographic's material interests." },
          { name: "Constituent Service & Local Problem-Solving Visibility", tag: "careful", why: "A 63% margin in a wealthy, educated district is not automatic — it reflects constituent confidence that the incumbent is actively serving the community's interests. A shift toward complacency, or a pattern of constituent concerns going unaddressed, is sufficient to erode support among voters sophisticated enough to organize an alternative. The incumbent must maintain visible, ongoing constituent engagement and be able to point to specific local problems solved; the challenger must identify real governance gaps and credibly offer solutions." },
          { name: "Treating 63% as an Irreversible Electoral Lock", tag: "avoid", why: "A 63-37 result in a district with nearly 40% college-educated adults is a comfortable margin, but not a guarantee. Affluent, educated constituencies evaluate on policy substance and constituent service, not partisan loyalty, and they have the resources and sophistication to organize an alternative if they conclude the incumbent is underperforming. Complacency, inattentiveness to local issues, or policy failures that directly affect homeowners' financial interests are sufficient to shift even a seemingly safe seat." },
          { name: "Abstract Conservative Positioning Without Local Policy Specificity", tag: "avoid", why: "A constituency with 39.5% college attainment and $99,643 median income does not respond to generic partisan messaging — they evaluate on substantive policy specificity and demonstrable track record. Any candidate, incumbent or challenger, who relies on abstract conservative or progressive framing without grounding it in specific, locally relevant policy proposals on education, taxes, or infrastructure will appear disconnected from the actual governance concerns that move this demographic." }
