          { name: "Suburban Homeowner Economic Security", tag: "lean-into", why: "The 72.6% homeownership rate and $84,981 median income signal a solidly middle-class homeowner constituency for whom property taxes, school funding, and the cost of maintaining suburban infrastructure are the primary governance concerns. At 41.0 median age, this is not an aging community but a stable, asset-holding one with real stakes in property values and local service quality. A representative who can demonstrate concrete results on tax fairness, school performance, and infrastructure maintenance consolidates the homeowner base and demonstrates why the unopposed margin reflects genuine constituent satisfaction rather than voter suppression." },
          { name: "Education Attainment and Professional Workforce", tag: "careful", why: "The 31.9% college attainment rate — above state averages — indicates a constituency with professional employment, higher educational expectations, and likely exposure to policy debates around higher education funding, student loan accessibility, and workforce development. This educated minority can become a swing factor if they perceive a representative as dismissive of education policy or hostile to public institution investment. Authentic engagement on higher education quality and affordability, even in a district where homeowners may dominate, prevents this constituency from organizing alternative support." },
          { name: "Treating Unopposed Victory as Permanent Mandate", tag: "avoid", why: "An unopposed result in 2024 reflects either such complete incumbent dominance that no challenger emerged, or such weak opposition recruitment that the seat appeared uncompetitive — but does not reveal the underlying sentiment of a 20% Hispanic constituency, a growing renter population, or any cross-pressured voter group. Assuming the unopposed margin persists without active constituent engagement and demonstrated results is a pathway to unexpected vulnerability in the next cycle when opposition inevitably rebuilds." },
          { name: "Demographic Change as Irrelevant to Strategy", tag: "avoid", why: "The 20.08% Hispanic share and 27.4% renter rate in a district where homeownership and Anglo identity might otherwise dominate represent real demographic shift. Campaigning as though the district's demographic composition is static — treating Hispanic and renter constituencies as peripheral rather than substantial — misreads the district's actual composition and invites a future challenger to mobilize these communities around responsive governance the incumbent neglected to demonstrate." }
