          { name: "Assuming Renter Constituency Loyalty", tag: "avoid", why: "A 50.1% renter constituency at $67,049 median income is not inherently Democratic — it is contingently Democratic insofar as Democratic governance delivers renter protections and housing cost containment. If Fortier's legislative record on housing affordability is perceived as insufficient or if a challenger emerges with more aggressive housing policy proposals, renter voters can shift. Treating the renter majority as a secure base rather than an actively engaged constituency requiring continuous demonstration of material benefit is a strategic error." },
          { name: "Ignoring the 44% Opposition as Unrecoverable", tag: "avoid", why: "In a district where median income is only $67,049 and 50% are renters, the 44% Republican opposition is unlikely to be purely ideological — it likely reflects white working-class voters with different economic priorities, older homeowners, or constituents frustrated with specific service failures. Any incumbent who campaigns this seat as though 56% is a secure majority without understanding what generated the 44% opposition is arithmetically wrong and strategically vulnerable to a challenger who can build a coalition around alternative economic messaging." }
