          { name: "Affluent Suburban Education & Healthcare Excellence", tag: "lean-into", why: "With 38% college-educated adults and $104,339 median household income, CT-HD-83 is a constituency that evaluates on whether schools are excellent, healthcare is accessible, and whether government is delivering equitable opportunity across the district. The 45% opposition in a high-income, educated district suggests material dissatisfaction with incumbent performance on these quality-of-life issues; a legislative agenda that treats education funding, school quality, and healthcare access as primary priorities — not secondary to tax policy — will resonate more credibly with the swing voters who delivered a narrow 55% margin." },
          { name: "Hispanic & Asian Professional Communities", tag: "careful", why: "The combined 19.45% Hispanic and Asian population in an affluent suburb includes both service-sector workers and professional households, many of whom are likely renters or younger homeowners evaluating on economic mobility and whether governance is equitable. These communities are internally diverse on economic concerns and potentially overlapping with the renter constituency; treating them as a unified voting bloc without attention to education, housing affordability, and professional opportunity will miss the opportunity to build support among swing voters of color who likely contributed to the 45% opposition." },
          { name: "Treating 55% as a Safe Majority", tag: "avoid", why: "A 55% result in a district with $104,339 median income, 38% college education, and 44.8 median age is not a safe Republican margin — it is a narrow plurality in a district that demographically should produce 60%+ results. The incumbent's failure to consolidate renters, younger professionals, and minority communities in an affluent, educated suburb signals that the 55% is based on homeowner alignment rather than durable coalition-building; any candidate who assumes this margin is stable or treats the seat as safe is misreading the district's actual competitive terrain." },
          { name: "Ignoring the Renter Constituency's Material Concerns", tag: "avoid", why: "In a district where renters are 17.1% of the population and likely include younger professionals and families in an affluent area, failing to address housing affordability, education quality, and economic mobility is ceding a persuadable swing constituency to the opposition. Any candidate whose message is structured around homeowner fiscal conservatism and property tax concerns without credible engagement on renter economic issues will reproduce Fazzino's narrow result and remain vulnerable to a challenger who speaks to the 45% opposition's actual priorities." }
