          { name: "Education and Economic Opportunity for a Diverse, College-Educated Constituency", tag: "lean-into", why: "With 46.9% college-educated adults — the highest attainment likely in this district's urban geography — and a racially diverse population (48.08% white, 27.44% Black, 17.34% Hispanic), this district prioritizes education access, workforce development, and pathways to economic mobility. The $81,559 median income suggests professional and working-professional households whose economic security depends on sustained education investment and job-market access. For the 27.44% Black and 17.34% Hispanic residents, education equity and opportunity are both economic concerns and signals of whether the district's institutions serve all residents fairly. A representative who articulates a clear agenda on school funding, higher education access, workforce training, and equitable economic development demonstrates alignment with this constituency's material priorities." },
          { name: "Equitable Service Delivery to a Racially Diverse Urban Constituency", tag: "careful", why: "A 91% Democratic result from a district that is 48.08% white, 27.44% Black, and 17.34% Hispanic signals strong support across racial communities — but requires that the representative actively sustains that cross-racial coalition through visible, consistent engagement with each community's specific concerns. Black and Hispanic residents in urban districts evaluate based on whether their neighborhoods receive equitable municipal investment, whether police practices and criminal justice are fair, and whether economic development reaches their communities. A representative who achieves 91% by consolidating white college-educated voters while neglecting Black and Hispanic constituent concerns risks erosion of the cross-racial coalition that generated the supermajority." },
          { name: "Treating 91% as Permission to Ignore Constituent Service or Shift Away From District Values", tag: "avoid", why: "A supermajority result invites the complacency of assuming electoral safety — the most common error that transforms a strong incumbent into a vulnerable one. In a college-educated, racially diverse urban district, voters expect sustained responsiveness and policy alignment; a representative who treats 91% as license to deprioritize casework, neglect minority community concerns, or shift toward positions misaligned with the district's progressive or urban-oriented values creates opening for a primary challenger who better reflects the constituency. The 9% Republican floor means there is no Republican threat to this seat, but it also means the entire electoral risk is internal to the Democratic coalition." },
          { name: "Defensive Positioning on Partisan or Ideological Grounds in a Consolidated Democratic District", tag: "avoid", why: "In a 91-9 district, conventional competitive campaign messaging — arguing against Republican alternatives, emphasizing partisan loyalty, or positioning on abstract ideological grounds — is misaligned with electoral reality and constituent expectations. The representative's attention and resources should focus on constituent service, visible local governance effectiveness, and sustained engagement with the diverse communities that comprise the coalition. A campaign message that emphasizes party loyalty or partisan conflict rather than local delivery and community responsiveness misreads the district's character and risks appearing tone-deaf to a constituency that already provides overwhelming support and expects that support to be met with attentiveness and effectiveness." }
