          { name: "Black Economic Mobility & Wealth-Building", tag: "lean-into", why: "The 45.3% Black population is the district's largest demographic group and the foundation of the 93% Democratic alignment, but Black households face accumulated wealth gaps and homeownership barriers that require specific legislative attention beyond partisan loyalty. Fair lending enforcement, small business support, predatory lending prohibition, and economic development policy targeted at wealth-building are the substantive issues that deepen the relationship with Black constituents and reflect confidence in their economic futures. The 93% margin indicates trust; converting that trust into material economic improvement requires specific policy delivery on the issues that shape Black household economic security." },
          { name: "Young Adult & Renter Turnout Engagement", tag: "careful", why: "The 30.4 median age is the youngest in typical state representative districts, and the 56.9% renter rate means the constituency skews toward younger adults facing economic mobility and housing stability challenges. Young renters are less likely to vote in non-presidential cycles and may disengage if they perceive that their economic concerns are not being addressed; maintaining the 93% Democratic alignment requires active turnout contact and demonstrated responsiveness to the housing, healthcare, and wage issues that shape young adult economic lives. Assuming young voter turnout will hold without active engagement is a strategic miscalculation in a district where demographic change and economic pressure create genuine volatility." },
          { name: "Treating 93% as Permission for Inattention to Constituent Economic Concerns", tag: "avoid", why: "A 93% partisan result is not a license to focus on abstract ideological priorities or state-level Democratic agenda items at the expense of the concrete housing, healthcare, and economic mobility issues that determine whether HD-94's renter majority and working families can sustain economic security. The 93% reflects trust accumulated through delivery on constituent concerns, not an endorsement of a generic Democratic legislative agenda. Any representative who allows the margin to become an excuse for reduced constituent service or inattention to housing and economic security will find that trust eroded in the next cycle or face a primary challenge from within the Democratic base." },
          { name: "Allowing Hispanic and Asian Communities to Become Invisible", tag: "avoid", why: "The 20% Hispanic and 7% Asian populations represent meaningful constituencies whose economic concerns—wage and hour enforcement, immigrant community support, healthcare access, small business development—can become obscured when the dominant narrative is Black majority politics. Neglecting these communities' specific constituent needs means governing 73% of the district with active engagement and leaving 27% of residents with the implicit message that their concerns are secondary. In a multiethnic district, this creates both a governance failure and an electoral risk: disengaged constituencies can become mobilized by a challenger who offers specific attention to their economic and community needs." }
