        issues: [
          { name: "Hispanic/Latino Economic Mobility & Renter-Community Issues", tag: "lean-into", why: "At 25.13% of the district, Hispanic/Latino voters are mathematically the swing constituency in a race decided by 8 points. With 45.8% renters district-wide and Hispanic households concentrated in the renter population, housing affordability, wages, and workplace protections are the primary economic drivers for this community. Any candidate who wins or holds CT-104 must demonstrate concrete policy commitment to rental housing affordability and economic opportunity for Hispanic working families — not token acknowledgment but measurable legislative track record or credible commitments on rent stabilization, wage floors, and workplace enforcement." },
          { name: "Working-Class Economic Security Across Racial Lines", tag: "lean-into", why: "The 45.8% renter rate, $73,864 median income, and 41.1-year median age together describe a constituency of working-age households with limited housing equity or long-term financial security. The combination of white (51.24%), Hispanic (25.13%), and Black (19.06%) communities creates a potential cross-racial working-class coalition if messaging centers material economic concerns rather than identity politics. A candidate who speaks to housing affordability, wage growth, healthcare access, and childcare costs as shared problems across racial lines, and who demonstrates legislative commitment to these issues, can build a durable majority in a district where economic precarity is the dominant lived reality." },
          { name: "College Attainment Gap & Workforce Development", tag: "careful", why: "At 24.8% college-educated adults, CT-104 has the lowest college attainment in the data set and signals a constituency where traditional four-year degree pathways are not the primary route to economic mobility. Vocational training, community college access, apprenticeship programs, and employer-sponsored skills development are the real opportunity structures for working-age adults without bachelor's degrees. Any candidate who positions education policy primarily around four-year universities without addressing community college affordability and workforce certification pathways is speaking past the constituency that actually lives here and needs to hear about accessible skill-building." },
          { name: "Treating 54% as a Comfortable Majority", tag: "avoid", why: "An 8-point margin in a district with this demographic diversity and renter concentration is not a safe incumbent position — it reflects a coalition that requires active maintenance across three major racial communities, all of whom are evaluating on economic delivery. Any incumbent or challenger who treats 54% as a floor rather than a contested result vulnerable to shifts in Hispanic turnout, Black voter enthusiasm, or working-class cross-racial mobilization will find themselves surprised by a smaller margin or a loss in the next cycle. This district demands year-round constituent engagement and measurable policy delivery, not complacency." },
          { name: "Identity-Based Messaging Without Economic Content", tag: "avoid", why: "In a district where 45.8% are renters, median income is $73,864, and college attainment is 24.8%, abstract messaging about diversity or representation without specific economic policy commitments will not mobilize or retain the Hispanic, Black, and working-class white voters who determine electoral outcomes. These communities evaluate candidates on whether government delivers material improvements in housing, wages, healthcare access, and opportunity — not on whether politicians acknowledge their existence. Any campaign that relies on identity rhetoric without pairing it to concrete economic policy proposals will underperform the incumbent's current 54% baseline." }
        ],
        memoHeadline: "Connecticut's 104th: A 54-46 Democratic Hold in a Diverse, Renter-Heavy District Where Hispanic Growth and Economic Mobility Are the Decisive Electoral Factors",
        memoParagraphs: [
          "CT-HD-104 is a genuinely competitive district that Rochelle won with only 54% in 2024 — a margin thin enough that modest shifts in turnout or candidate performance among any major constituency group could flip the seat. The district's demographic profile reveals why: 51.24% white, 25.13% Hispanic/Latino, 19.06% Black, with a 45.8% renter rate and median household income of $73,864. This is not a homeowner-dominant, economically secure district — nearly half the households are renters, and the median income, while not distressed, sits at the national middle without cushion. The 24.8% college attainment rate is the lowest signal in the data and suggests a constituency where higher education access and workforce development directly shape economic mobility. The 41.1-year median age indicates a mix of working-age families and younger adults, many of whom are evaluating candidates on whether government is delivering tangible economic security, not inherited partisan loyalty.",
          "The Hispanic/Latino community at 25.13% — one-quarter of the district — is the single most important swing factor in CT-HD-104's electoral future. In a district where the incumbent won by 8 points and renters comprise 45.8% of households, Hispanic renter households are a constituency where economic anxiety, housing affordability, and workplace conditions are the primary political drivers. Whether the Democratic hold at 54% is durable depends almost entirely on whether Hispanic and Black voters perceive sustained benefit from Democratic representation on the material issues that shape their daily lives: housing costs in a high-renter district, wages and workplace conditions, and access to services. The white majority at 51.24% is not monolithic — a substantial portion are renters and working-class households whose economic interests may not align with a comfortable suburban Republican base. For a Republican challenger, the path to 50%+ runs through consolidating white working-class and renter households while peeling Hispanic voters away from Democratic performance by offering specific, credible economic policy alternatives. For Rochelle or any Democratic incumbent, holding 54% requires delivering measurable progress on the economic issues that dominate renter and Hispanic household priorities."
        ],
        memoBullets: [
          "Target the Hispanic/Latino community (25.13%) with specificity on housing affordability, wage growth, and workplace conditions — this is the swing constituency that determines whether a 54% result holds or erodes; generic appeals to 'diversity' will not mobilize or retain these voters.",
          "Lead on the renter crisis: at 45.8% renters in a district where median income is $73,864, housing affordability is not a peripheral issue but a central economic security concern for nearly half the electorate — any candidate who neglects renter-specific policy (rent stabilization, tenant protections, affordable housing development) is ceding ground to an opponent.",
          "Engage the white working-class and renter majority within the 51.24% white population as a distinct constituency from suburban homeowners — their economic interests around wages, housing, and service access align more closely with Hispanic and Black renters than with affluent homeowners, and messaging should reflect that alignment rather than assuming monolithic white voting behavior.",
          "Address the low college attainment rate (24.8%) directly through workforce development, vocational training, and community college access — this signals a constituency where pathways to economic mobility beyond four-year degrees are the lived reality, and policy specificity on this dimension outperforms abstract education rhetoric."
        ]
