          { name: "Education Quality & State Funding", tag: "lean-into", why: "With 54.3% college attainment, CT-HD-107 is a district where education is not an abstract policy area but a lived family reality — parents actively evaluating school quality and state investment in K-12 and higher education as direct measures of government competence. The 44.9 median age and high income level together signal a constituency of professionals in peak earning and child-rearing years whose property tax burden is substantial and whose willingness to pay it depends on visible school quality and strategic state investment in education. This is the policy terrain where Democratic messaging is gaining traction in affluent suburban districts nationally, and it is the primary swing issue within the homeowner base." },
          { name: "Understanding the 47% Opposition", tag: "careful", why: "The 47% Democratic share in a high-income, heavily homeowner, 76.84% white district is a red flag that something meaningful is driving opposition beyond normal partisan baseline — whether specific education funding concerns, tax policy disputes, healthcare cost anxiety, or social policy positions on which college-educated voters diverge from Republican alignment. The incumbent won with 53%, which is a plurality, not a commanding majority in a district this affluent and educated. Any candidate must specifically diagnose what is driving that 47% rather than treating it as base Democratic votes to be managed; ignoring or misunderstanding these swing voters is the path to losing a district that should be safer." },
          { name: "Treating 53% as a Durable Republican Floor", tag: "avoid", why: "A 53-47 result in a district with 54.3% college-educated adults, $151K income, and 76.84% white population is not a stable margin — it is a margin achieved by consolidation of homeowner Republicans while losing ground to Democrats among college-educated professionals. Affluent suburban districts nationwide are the fastest-shifting terrain in contemporary American politics, and the combination of high education, high income, and strong Democratic opposition is precisely the profile of districts flipping from Republican to Democratic. Any candidate who assumes this seat is safe because the incumbent won 53% in a polarized 2024 cycle is misreading the underlying demographic transition." },
          { name: "Ignoring Healthcare Costs & Tax Burden", tag: "avoid", why: "Affluent professionals at $151K income with 11.9% renter rates are homeowners with substantial property tax burdens and aging-parent healthcare cost concerns, and these material economic issues are the terrain where Democratic messaging has gained traction in similar districts. Any candidate who campaigns on abstract conservative principles rather than addressing specific concerns about healthcare system performance, insurance costs, and whether state tax and service policy delivers measurable value is ceding the swing constituency's decision calculus to the opposition. In a district this affluent, voters will evaluate based on whether government is working for them — and 47% currently conclude it is not." }
