          { name: "Affluent Suburban Stability & Quality-of-Life Preservation", tag: "lean-into", why: "CT-HD-117 is one of Connecticut's most affluent districts: $119,092 median household income, 44.1% college-educated, 84.38% white, and only 16.3% renter rate signal a stable, asset-holding, homeowner-dominant constituency with high material investment in the status quo. The 52% Democratic margin in a district this wealthy and educated is not a landslide — it reflects a constituency that evaluates on quality of life, property values, schools, and fiscal management rather than partisan ideology. Any candidate must speak specifically to the infrastructure, schools, property tax management, and environmental stewardship that preserve the district's affluent suburban character and justify continued investment in its governance." },
          { name: "College-Educated Swing Constituency & Credential-Based Persuasion", tag: "lean-into", why: "At 44.1% college-educated — significantly above state average — CT-HD-117 is a district where voters evaluate candidates on expertise, policy depth, and demonstrated competence rather than partisan affiliation or charisma alone. The 52-48 margin in a district this educated suggests that four percentage points represent college-educated voters who are genuinely persuadable based on candidate quality, specific policy answers, and demonstrated understanding of local governance challenges. A candidate who can articulate evidence-based solutions to the district's real problems — school funding, infrastructure maintenance, tax efficiency — and who demonstrates substantive command of their portfolio significantly outperforms a candidate relying on partisan messaging or generalized platitudes." },
          { name: "Demographic Diversity Gap & Minority Community Integration", tag: "careful", why: "The reported demographics account for 99.89% of the population, leaving only a ~0.11% gap, but the 84.38% white share paired with modest Hispanic (8.72%), Asian (2.95%), and Black (2.84%) populations signals a district in demographic transition toward greater diversity. In an affluent suburban Connecticut district, the integration of growing minority populations into the civic infrastructure, school systems, and economic opportunity framework is both a governance obligation and a latent political vulnerability. A candidate who treats minority communities as appendages rather than full constituents of the affluent suburban project risks organizing opposition among voters who perceive exclusion from the quality-of-life benefits the district offers." },
          { name: "Partisan Loyalty in a Genuinely Competitive Seat", tag: "avoid", why: "A 52-48 result is not a Democratic hold — it is a contested seat where 48% actively chose the Republican candidate in a district with above-average education and income. Any Democratic incumbent who campaigns this seat as though the 52% represents a durable majority, rather than a narrow plurality in a persuadable constituency, is misreading the district's actual political alignment. The affluence and education of CT-HD-117 correlate with genuine ticket-splitting and candidate-specific evaluation; assuming partisan loyalty in a 52-48 district is a path to defeat." },
          { name: "Treating Fiscal Management as a Secondary Issue", tag: "avoid", why: "In a district with $119,092 median income, 84% homeownership, and demonstrated fiscal sophistication, dismissing property tax fairness, public pension sustainability, and government efficiency as 'Republican talking points' rather than addressing them substantively is a strategic error. Affluent homeowners in Connecticut are not reflexively partisan; they are fiscal stakeholders in local government. A candidate who deflects on budget accountability or frames tax efficiency as ideological rather than material governance is ceding credibility to an opponent who takes these issues seriously." }
