          { name: "Workplace Standards and Economic Security for Working-Class Communities", tag: "lean-into", why: "The Hispanic majority (53.46%) concentrated in service, construction, and agricultural sectors and the Black community (30.17%) in service and wage-labor sectors together form a constituency whose primary economic concern is stable, above-poverty wages and workplace protections — not credential-based mobility. Minimum wage floors that track cost of living, workplace safety enforcement, and protections against wage theft and unfair labor practices are the material governance issues for this constituency. A representative who speaks with specificity about worker protections and wage standards commands more credibility with this coalition than one who defaults to education or entrepreneurship messaging that assumes educational pathways are equally accessible to all." },
          { name: "Healthcare Access and Equity for Hispanic and Black Communities", tag: "careful", why: "At 53.46% Hispanic and 30.17% Black, the district includes working communities with documented disparities in healthcare access, maternal health outcomes, and chronic disease management — and specific cultural and linguistic barriers to navigating healthcare systems. The Black community faces additional equity concerns around provider bias and historical medical trauma. Healthcare messaging that centers equity and access rather than cost containment or insurance expansion alone better resonates with these constituencies' actual concerns. However, messaging must be specific to each community's documented needs; generic 'healthcare access' rhetoric without acknowledgment of equity dimensions can feel distant from constituents' lived experience." },
          { name: "Treating 71% as a Durable Base Rather Than Contingent Coalition", tag: "avoid", why: "The 71% margin reflects strong Democratic alignment, but the alignment is built on delivery on material economic issues — housing, wages, healthcare — not on partisan loyalty or cultural values consensus. An incumbent who campaigns as though 71% is a stable floor and who reduces constituent engagement to election cycles rather than sustained delivery on concrete issues is vulnerable to a challenger who can credibly claim the incumbent has neglected the coalition's material needs. This constituency will reward delivery and punish neglect; the margin is only as durable as the representative's ongoing responsiveness to economic concerns." },
          { name: "Defaulting to Education-Expansion Messaging for Low College-Attainment Constituency", tag: "avoid", why: "With only 12.1% college attainment, CT-HD-124's working-class majority evaluates on what government delivers for people without college degrees, not on abstract education-expansion messaging that can feel condescending or irrelevant to immediate economic needs. A representative whose primary answer to economic insecurity is 'get a degree' misreads the constituency and cedes credibility to any challenger who offers concrete wage, housing, and healthcare solutions. College pathways may be part of a broader economic opportunity agenda, but leading with education in a low-attainment district with immediate affordability pressures signals distance from constituents' actual priorities." }
