          { name: "Labor Standards & Wage Growth for Service & Immigrant-Majority Workforce", tag: "lean-into", why: "The 34.15% Hispanic plurality and the district's urban economy centered on service, hospitality, and light manufacturing means a substantial portion of the constituency is in low-to-moderate wage employment. Minimum wage policy, paid leave, workplace protections for immigrant workers, and access to healthcare and benefits are material economic issues that directly affect household stability. The 38.3% college attainment suggests a two-tier economy: college-educated professionals alongside working-class service and immigrant households. Governing for both means advancing wage standards and worker protections that improve conditions for the less-credentialed portion of the constituency alongside education and mobility pathways for those pursuing credentials." },
          { name: "Consolidation as Ongoing Work, Not Assumed Durability", tag: "careful", why: "While 76% appears safe, renter-majority urban constituencies are more volatile than homeowner-dominant ones because housing cost burden is higher and economic disruption cascades faster through lower-income households. The 24% Republican opposition is not persuadable, but the 76% Democratic base can fracture if constituent expectations on housing, wage, or service issues go unmet. An incumbent who assumes the margin is durable rather than earned risks discovering that a credible challenger offering concrete material improvements can mobilize the 10-15% of current supporters who are evaluated the incumbent on delivery rather than inherited partisan identity. Consolidation requires continuous constituent engagement and visible legislative outcomes on housing and economic security." },
          { name: "Positioning the District as Ideologically Monolithic", tag: "avoid", why: "While the 76% is decisively Democratic, the constituency is not unified by ideology — it is unified by material economic interests and the perception that Democratic representation advances those interests. An incumbent who campaigns on abstract progressive ideological positions rather than concrete delivery on renter housing security, wage standards, and service access risks misaligning with the coalition's primary evaluative criterion, which is whether government is making their lives materially better." },
          { name: "Taking the 62% Renter Majority for Granted", tag: "avoid", why: "Renters are more likely than homeowners to move, have fewer fixed assets anchoring them to community, and are more acutely affected by economic disruption. An incumbent who provides responsive constituent service and legislative focus on housing affordability to renters will find they are more loyal and more active in turnout than the raw partisan margin suggests; an incumbent who assumes renter support as given and focuses energy elsewhere will discover that the 76% is more fragile than it appears. This constituency requires active engagement and demonstrated delivery on the issues that keep them economically stable." }
