          { name: "Working-Age Employment and Wage Standards", tag: "lean-into", why: "The 34.5 median age and 17.5% college attainment indicate a working-age population competing in lower-wage labor markets where job availability, wage floors, and workplace protections directly determine household economic stability — not abstract career trajectories. Constituents in CT-HD-130 evaluate representation on whether jobs are available, whether wages are sufficient to cover basics, and whether workplaces enforce safety and hour standards; state-level policies on minimum wage, wage theft enforcement, and occupational safety are direct material governance levers that affect most households in this district. An incumbent or challenger who engages specifically on working-age constituent employment concerns with legislative specificity commands far more credibility than one who defaults to generic economic messaging." },
          { name: "Healthcare Access and Cost for Low-Income Urban Population", tag: "careful", why: "With $40,786 median income and high rates of uninsured and underinsured residents, healthcare access and cost are constituent concerns that can shift political evaluation rapidly — emergency room wait times, prescription drug costs, and preventive care availability determine both health outcomes and household financial stability. Healthcare debates at the state level are most persuasive when grounded in specific local solutions (ED overcrowding, clinic accessibility, insurance program access) rather than national partisan framing; a representative who can point to specific constituency healthcare improvements has leverage with this population, but one who engages healthcare only in abstract terms will find constituents skeptical of whether government is actually helping them." },
          { name: "Taking 80% as Durable Partisan Strength Rather Than Current Performance Validation", tag: "avoid", why: "An 80% result in a low-income, high-renter urban district should be interpreted as validation of current incumbent performance and economic conditions, not as evidence of durable Democratic partisan strength or as permission to reduce constituent engagement. This is not a district where the opposition is permanently organized against you; it is a district where material deterioration or perceived neglect of the working-poor constituency can create an opening for a challenger very quickly. Assuming the 80% is safe and reducing active constituent presence or responsiveness will be tactically disastrous when economic conditions worsen or when organized opposition emerges." },
          { name: "Substituting Abstract Policy Debate for Material Economic Governance", tag: "avoid", why: "At 17.5% college attainment and $40,786 median income, constituents in CT-HD-130 evaluate representation on concrete material grounds — Can I afford my rent? Are jobs available? Is the emergency room accessible? — not on ideological positioning or abstract policy proposals. A representative who engages this constituency primarily through partisan framing or national policy debates rather than through specific, locally grounded governance solutions on housing, employment, and healthcare will find credibility eroding rapidly, regardless of partisan advantage. This is a constituency that will reward a challenger who can demonstrate concrete material improvement in their economic conditions far more readily than one who assumes partisan loyalty as a substitute for consistent delivery." }
