          "Lead on cost-of-living concrete solutions: at $100,800 income with 32.1% renters and college-educated professionals, this constituency evaluates on housing affordability, property tax fairness, and whether government is delivering tangible relief from the inflation and cost pressures that drove the narrow 51% result.",
          "Do not assume college-educated voters are secure Democratic votes: 38% college attainment in a $100,800-income district creates a constituency vulnerable to business-friendly messaging, especially if the incumbent or challenger can speak credibly to economic growth and reduced regulatory burden without appearing to abandon social policy concerns.",
          "Treat 51-49 as the baseline reality, not evidence of a secure hold: a one-point margin in a district with significant demographic diversity and a 32.1% renter rate signals active persuasion remains possible; any candidate who consolidates existing coalition without actively engaging the opposition's economic concerns is at risk of losing ground in the next cycle."
