          { name: "High-Income Household Economic Security & Wealth Preservation", tag: "lean-into", why: "At $250,001 median household income—among the highest in Connecticut—this constituency's primary governance concerns are property tax management, estate planning frameworks, healthcare quality for an aging professional class, and retirement security. These are not abstract conservative principles; they are material economic interests that shape how affluent households evaluate their representatives. A candidate who leads with specific, competent policy on these issues commands the credibility this constituency requires and differentiates from opposition messaging that may be resonating on separate grounds." },
          { name: "Intellectual Rigor & Evidence-Based Policy Communication", tag: "lean-into", why: "The 85.7% college-educated adult population is the highest education concentration in the state—a constituency of professionals, managers, and executives accustomed to evidence-based reasoning in their own work who expect equivalent intellectual rigor from elected officials. Generic partisan rhetoric or unsourced claims will actively alienate this audience; policy proposals must be grounded in specific data, realistic implementation timelines, and acknowledgment of trade-offs. A candidate who can speak competently and specifically to complex policy problems gains substantial advantage with this demographic, while one who relies on partisan talking points will find the opposition's counter-arguments more credible." },
          { name: "Asian and Hispanic Professional Community Engagement", tag: "careful", why: "While numerically smaller (6.47% Asian, 5.13% Hispanic), these populations in a high-income district include business owners, executives, and professionals whose economic interests may align with Republican governance but whose civic and cultural concerns require genuine constituent engagement. These communities can become invisible in a district coded as overwhelmingly white, but their needs around business regulation, discrimination policy, and cultural recognition are real; authentic engagement with these constituencies, rather than assumption that their economic interests alone will hold their loyalty, is necessary for sustainable electoral performance." },
          { name: "Assuming 58% Represents a Durable Majority", tag: "avoid", why: "In a district this wealthy and educated, 58% is a clear win but not a commanding mandate—and the 42% opposition in a Republican-lean Connecticut district suggests either that specific governance issues are driving voters to opposition, or that meaningful swing voters are evaluating the seat on competence rather than partisan loyalty. Any incumbent or challenger who treats this margin as a settled base rather than as a signal of selective vulnerability and investigates what is driving the 42% will be strategically disadvantaged." },
          { name: "Partisan Identity Over Competence in a Knowledge-Worker Constituency", tag: "avoid", why: "A constituency where 85.7% hold college degrees and the median household income is $250,001 does not vote primarily on partisan identity; these voters evaluate on perceived competence, specific policy outcomes, and whether their representative understands the material economic concerns of high-income households. Any candidate whose campaign strategy relies on energizing partisan base voters rather than demonstrating specific economic competence and governance capability will fail to compete effectively for the persuadable portion of this affluent, educated electorate." }
