          { name: "Education Quality, Access & Racial Equity", tag: "lean-into", why: "At 57.1% college-educated adults, education is both a defining feature of the district's professional character and a direct policy priority for families with school-age children and younger adults entering the workforce. In a diverse district (59.53% white, 16.74% Hispanic, 14.87% Black, 5.67% Asian), education policy that delivers excellence for existing high-performers while genuinely improving access and outcomes for underserved communities—particularly students of color and working-class families—addresses both the district's affluence and its demographic complexity. School funding, racial equity in discipline and tracking, and the pathway from secondary education to economic opportunity are the concrete policy questions a constituency this educated will evaluate on substance." },
          { name: "Hispanic & Asian Community Economic Mobility", tag: "careful", why: "The 16.74% Hispanic and 5.67% Asian populations in an affluent, educated district represent electorally significant communities whose economic and civic engagement cannot be assumed to follow white professional or existing Black community voting patterns. Hispanic workers in Connecticut often occupy service and skilled trades sectors; Asian professionals bring high educational attainment but may face specific barriers to senior leadership and wealth accumulation. Both communities evaluate on concrete economic mobility, education access for their children, and whether they experience inclusive civic participation—not symbolic representation. A candidate's relationship with these communities is contingent on specific policy delivery and authentic leadership engagement, not demographic proportionality in campaign materials." },
          { name: "Assuming Unopposed Status Indicates Safe Majority", tag: "avoid", why: "The 2024 unopposed result reflects coalition-building success, not structural Republican strength or electoral inevitability—it means Dathan has neutralized opposition through constituent service and policy delivery sufficient that no credible challenger emerged. In a district this educated, diverse, and affluent, that consensus is contingent on continued performance; a significant failure on housing, education equity, or responsive governance can quickly activate opposition. Any candidate who treats 100% as a floor rather than a current achievement will underestimate the electoral risk in a future cycle." },
          { name: "Taking White Professional Base for Granted", tag: "avoid", why: "At 59.53% white in an affluent, highly educated district, the white professional constituency is the largest demographic bloc—but it is not an inherited majority in a district where education and income correlate with issue-based voting, not partisan loyalty. White professionals in CT-HD-142 vote on education quality, housing policy, environmental protection, and tax fairness; a candidate who assumes this group will support based on party label while neglecting policy substance on these issues will find support eroding to a credible alternative. This constituency demands policy specificity, not demographic assumption." }
