          "Hispanic and Asian community engagement is a swing variable—the 16.74% Hispanic and 5.67% Asian populations in an affluent, educated district are electorally significant and actively evaluating on economic mobility, education quality, and whether government is responsive to their specific needs. These communities are not monolithic; treating them as appendages to a white professional base misses the opportunity to build durable majority support.",
          "Address housing affordability and renter economic security directly—the 31.4% renter rate in a $133,342-income district signals a meaningful population of younger professionals, service workers, and families for whom housing costs are a primary economic constraint. Any candidate must offer specific policy remedies on housing supply, tenant protections, and the relationship between housing costs and economic opportunity.",
          "Lead on education quality and opportunity equity—57.1% college attainment in a professional, affluent constituency makes education both an indicator of district character and a direct policy priority. A candidate who frames education policy around both excellence for existing high-performers and genuine access and equity for underserved students (particularly Hispanic, Black, and Asian working-class families) engages the full demographic reality of the district."
