          { name: "Service Delivery & Constituent Responsiveness", tag: "lean-into", why: "Renter households and lower-income constituencies depend directly on public services—schools, transit, sanitation, public safety—for daily economic function and quality of life; these are not abstract policy concerns but material governance experiences that shape political behavior. An incumbent who demonstrates responsiveness to constituent service requests, visible investment in schools and transit, and accountability for service failures builds the trust necessary to maintain 83% in a district where alternatives always exist and where non-voting is a realistic option for frustrated constituents." },
          { name: "Communities of Color Political Engagement & Representation", tag: "careful", why: "At 65.5% communities of color—36.32% Black, 29.18% Hispanic, 4.75% Asian—the district's political economy is inseparable from racial equity concerns: police accountability, healthcare access disparities, education quality for students of color, and hiring equity in public employment are constituency priorities that shape whether voters experience government as serving them. An incumbent who engages these constituencies as distinct political blocs with specific policy needs rather than as a fungible coalition under a broader Democratic umbrella builds deeper political resilience; an incumbent who assumes voters of color will remain Democratic regardless of specific attention to their concerns is making a vulnerable strategic assumption." },
          { name: "Treating 83% as a Safe Supermajority", tag: "avoid", why: "An 83-17 result is not a durable supermajority floor in a district where 69.7% of households are renters facing direct economic pressure and where service delivery failures can be immediately politicized. If the incumbent fails to deliver material progress on rent, housing, or school/transit quality, non-voter activation rather than persuasion becomes the opposition's path to competitive viability. Assuming 83% is safe and reducing constituent engagement accordingly is the primary path to erosion in this district." },
          { name: "Framing Housing as a Special Interest Rather Than a Material Necessity", tag: "avoid", why: "Any incumbent rhetorical framing that positions affordable housing, rent control, or tenant protections as a 'special interest' priority rather than as a material economic necessity for two-thirds of the district will be actively hostile to constituent lived experience. In a district where most households are rent-burdened and where housing insecurity shapes health, education outcomes, and economic mobility, politicians who speak as though housing advocacy is an optional policy preference rather than a fundamental governing obligation are speaking against the material reality of their constituency." }
